# StartOS update packages

Every completed StartOS build must produce an **update-capable** `.s9pk`. StartOS only upgrades in place when the new package has a **higher version** than what is installed. Re-sideloading the same version forces an uninstall first.

## Required on every build

1. **Bump the version** before packing (downstream revision for wrapper-only changes):
   - `0.1.2:0` → `0.1.2:1` in the current file under `packages/start9/startos/versions/`
   - Edit `releaseNotes.en_US` to describe what changed
2. **Run the full build** from repo root:
   ```bash
   RELEASE_NOTES="Short summary of this build." ./packages/start9/build.sh
   ```
   `build.sh` calls `scripts/bump-version.sh` automatically unless `SKIP_VERSION_BUMP=1`.
3. **Output:** `packages/start9/erv-web_x86_64.s9pk` — this *is* the update package.

## Install as an update (no uninstall)

**UI:** StartOS → **Sideload** → upload the new `.s9pk` (higher version installs over the old one).

**CLI (same LAN):**
```bash
start-cli package install erv-web 0.1.2:1 --sideload packages/start9/erv-web_x86_64.s9pk
```

Use the version string printed at the end of the build (`erv-web vX.Y.Z:N`).

## When a migration is needed

Most ERV bumps are wrapper-only (web UI + server binary): bump downstream in the **same** version file.

If user data / store schema must migrate:
1. Rename the current version file to match the old version (e.g. `v0.1.2.0.ts`)
2. Add it to `other` in `startos/versions/index.ts`
3. Create a new current version file with `up` / `down` migrations

See [StartOS Versions docs](https://docs.start9.com/packaging/0.4.0.x/versions.html).

## Do not

- Ship a rebuild without bumping version when the user will sideload over an existing install
- Skip release notes on builds you expect the user to install
